  "worked_solution": "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $\u0394x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\u0394y={y}_{\\text{new}}^{\u2032}\\cdot \u0394x,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{\u2032}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\u0394x=2-0=2.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $\u0394y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u0394y & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{\u2032}\\cdot \u0394x \\\\  & =(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}-4{x}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}-8{x}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+\u0394y$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\u0394y \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}-8{x}_{\\text{new}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}-2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y-8{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ (1-2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}){y}_{\\text{new}} & =y-8{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y-8{x}_{\\text{new}}}{1-2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+\u0394x=0+2=2.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y-8{x}_{\\text{new}}}{1-2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-8\\cdot 2}{1-2\\cdot {2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{7} \\\\  & \u22482.14,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)\u22482.14.$",
